{
  "statement": "The diagnosis in this resolution is correct, and it's ugly: a person denied a mortgage or flagged by a risk score cannot prove what the algorithm did to them, because they're never allowed to open the box. Tort law was built for visible defects — a cracked axle, a leaking valve — not proprietary statistical machinery shielded by trade-secret claims. In the Loomis case in Wisconsin in 2016, a defendant sentenced partly on the COMPAS risk score was barred from inspecting how it scored him, because Northpointe called the formula a trade secret. That is the accountability gap, and it is real. So far the resolution and I agree.\n\nHere's where we part. A subject-keyed cryptographic audit trail is a receipt, not an explanation. It proves a log wasn't tampered with, and it records what went in and what came out. It does not tell you why. The decision logic of a deep learning model isn't reconstructable from a tamper-proof timestamp — the system produces emergent behavior its own builders can't fully narrate. Mandate the trail and you get exactly what disclosure mandates always produce: voluminous, certified, signal-poor artifacts that satisfy the statute and explain nothing. Congress would be ordering firms to hand victims a sealed, notarized envelope full of static.\n\nThe mandate itself is the capture surface. Whoever drafts the audit standard decides what counts as adequate — and that drafting table fills up with the companies being audited. We've watched this movie. Narrowly-scoped audits get used as deflection devices; firms commission a friendly assessor, pass a checklist, and wave the certificate at every lawsuit. Build a cryptographic attestation regime and you've also built a chokepoint — somebody powerful holds the keys and defines the schema. An accountability tool governed by the parties it audits is just a more expensive way to look accountable.\n\nSplit the proposal and the picture clarifies. Expanded discovery rights attack the actual disease — the information asymmetry that lets opacity defeat causation — and they deserve support, especially now. Federal officials are actively moving to dismantle disparate-impact liability under Title VII and the Fair Housing Act, the very doctrines that let plaintiffs challenge discriminatory outcomes without reading the code. Knock those hooks out and discovery becomes the last lever a victim has. But discovery only matters if the plaintiff can read what they pull. The legible question isn't 'do logs exist' — it's who can interpret them and who decides what they must reveal.\n\nLet me check my own claim before an opponent does. Not every firm evades, and not every standard gets captured — the cynicism cuts both ways, and 'assume the agency fails' is as lazy as 'assume the agency saves us.' The entire value of this statute lives in a variable the resolution leaves blank: whether independent, non-vendor third parties define audit adequacy, and whether private litigants can enforce it below a captured federal agency. Get that wrong and you've spent billions certifying the appearance of justice. So my question to both of my colleagues is simple. When the audit standard is finally written, who's sitting at that table — and who, exactly, isn't allowed in the room?",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🧾", "tooltip": "A cryptographic audit trail is a notarized receipt — it certifies that something happened, not that it was just or even readable."},
    {"symbol": "🔑", "tooltip": "A mandated attestation regime is a chokepoint, and the debate that matters is who gets to hold the keys."},
    {"symbol": "🚪", "tooltip": "Accountability dies at the door of the room where the standard gets written — and the audited firms are already seated inside."}
  ],
