  "statement": "The Skeptic has the diagnosis exactly right, and I want to start there because it matters. In State v. Loomis, a Wisconsin court let Northpointe shield the COMPAS risk algorithm behind a trade-secret claim, and the defendant never saw how the machine scored him. That is the disease: information asymmetry severs the chain of causation before a plaintiff can reach it. The Skeptic is also right that a cryptographic audit trail is a receipt, not an explanation. A tamper-proof log proves who issued what and when. It does not narrate why the model decided. I will not pretend otherwise, and any safetyist who does is selling you a printout dressed up as understanding.\n\nHere is where my evidence leads somewhere the Skeptic does not. The Skeptic wants to split the proposal: keep expanded discovery, discard the audit-trail mandate as wasteful. But the Skeptic already conceded the joint that defeats this — discovery only matters if the plaintiff can read what they pull. A standardized, tamper-proof record is precisely what makes a discovery request legible instead of a signal-poor data dump no litigant can interpret. Discovery without a standardized record yields opacity by volume. A record without discovery rights stays sealed under the next Loomis trade-secret claim. They are complements, not severable halves. What would have to be true for the split to work? You would need discovery to pierce opacity on its own — and Loomis is the proof it cannot.\n\nThis is not academic, because the last lever is being removed as we speak. Federal officials are actively dismantling disparate-impact liability under Title VII and the Fair Housing Act. When the statutory hooks for proving discrimination disappear, discovery becomes the final mechanism a wronged person has. A lever needs something solid to pull on. Strip the standardized record, and you hand plaintiffs a lever attached to nothing.\n\nNow take the audit trail on its own terms, narrowed honestly. Its value is not explaining the model. Its value is binding a named human fiduciary to a provable record of what was issued in their name. AI systems perform consequential acts — denying a loan, scoring a defendant, flagging a tenant — and today no human is bound to answer for them. The trail closes the deniability gap: who acted, what was issued, traceable to a person with a legal duty. It does not address an output that was harmful but legitimately issued. That job belongs to separate explainability mandates. I will not ask one tool to do two jobs. Keep humans responsible for what machines do in their name — that is the whole point of the anchor.\n\nThe Skeptic's strongest objection is that the mandate is itself the capture surface — whoever drafts the audit standard decides what counts as adequate, and that whoever is the audited firms. That objection is correct, and it is an argument for design specifics, not against the mandate. The Boeing 737 MAX killed 346 people because the FAA let Boeing self-certify the MCAS system. The lesson is not 'abandon certification.' The lesson is 'never let the audited party write the standard.' So the standard must be authored by independent non-vendor bodies, and enforced through a private right of action that survives below any captured federal agency. The Skeptic asks who is at the table when the standard is written. My answer: affected communities, independent third parties, and private litigants — not the firms being audited. That is the debate worth having, and a voluntary commitment is luck, not safety.",
