{
  "statement": "Skeptic is right about the thing that matters most: the mandate itself becomes the capture surface. Whoever drafts a national cryptographic audit schema decides what counts as adequate, and that drafting table fills up with the firms being audited. Skeptic's other point lands even harder — a subject-keyed cryptographic trail is a receipt, not an explanation. It can prove what a system did. It cannot tell you why. Safetyist's answer is the strongest version available: don't abandon certification, just never let the audited party write the standard, and bind the trail to a named human fiduciary enforced through a private right of action. The Boeing 737 MAX is the clean precedent — FAA self-certification killed 346 people across two crashes, and the lesson there was independence, not deregulation. I accept that lesson. Here is where my evidence diverges: that lesson argues for publicly-funded independent evaluation, which is my fix, not for a flat privately-borne mandate that prices out everyone too small to staff a compliance team.\n\nStart with what Skeptic already conceded, because it's the scoping rule nobody picked up. A receipt can't explain a model — fine. So stop assigning it explanatory work and regulate only what a cryptographic trail can actually verify. Provenance, mandated disclosure, signed action attestation, fabricated-citation checks against real source databases. The C2PA provenance standard is deployed today across Adobe, Microsoft, and major camera makers — it cryptographically marks content origin without adjudicating a single model's reasoning. A citation-fabrication check runs against a database; it returns true or false. None of this requires a court to read model logic. The honest limit: this procedural layer misses diffuse harms that no one ever litigates. I'm not pretending otherwise. But a checkable trigger that ships beats an explanation mandate that no one can enforce.\n\nNow the part both prior speakers missed, and it's the one that should worry this panel most. A flat federal cryptographic mandate is not one capture surface. It's two. Skeptic named the drafting table. I'm naming the cost. Large labs already run compliance teams — OpenAI and Google absorb a new schema as a line item. An open-source developer or a three-person lab eats it as a fixed entry toll. That toll doesn't scale to risk or revenue. It's a flat fee, and flat fees are regressive by definition. So the mandate sold as accountability funnels power to the exact incumbents it claims to police. The fix is publicly-funded, open-methodology evaluation infrastructure — many contestable tools, not one privately-borne schema.\n\nLet me preempt the obvious counter, because it's a good one. The EU AI Act carves out SME provisions, and NIST builds tiered frameworks — so proportionate compliance is achievable. Exactly. That's my point, not the rebuttal to it. Tiering proves the burden is a fixable design flaw. Which is precisely why the unstructured flat version in this resolution is indefensible. If you can tier it, an untiered mandate is a choice to concentrate, not an accident. And it compounds a problem already in the field: incumbents use contractual lock-in right now to block independent testing of their systems. A flat mandate doesn't cure that asymmetry. It hardens it.\n\nThe deepest tension is one neither speaker surfaced. Skeptic and Safetyist are fighting over who sits at the single standard-setting table. Neither asks whether there must be one table. The capture surface isn't a staffing problem — it's a property of centralization itself. So here's the open architecture question: if the chokepoint is what both prior speakers fear, why does the answer default to one congressional schema-setter? Decentralized verification and randomized, concealed audit timing distribute the keys instead of handing them to one body. I'll flag my own weakness — no one has run cryptographic verification of complex AI properties at production scale, and the oracle problem is real. So I offer that as a question, not a finished product. But the regressive-burden point and the procedural-scoping point stand on their own. The resolution leaps from 'tort has gaps' to 'sweeping cryptographic regime.' Expanded discovery plus a thin procedural layer may close most of that gap — and every year you spend building the cathedral instead, the asymmetry you're trying to fix keeps running.",
