{
  "gap_arguments": [
    {
      "argument": "The entire debate presumes the injured subject is a litigant who can afford to litigate; but a tamper-proof, court-legible record plus a private right of action is worthless to the modal victim — a low-income mortgage applicant or a criminal defendant with a public defender — who cannot fund expert witnesses to interpret a model log even if discovery hands it to them. Without publicly-funded litigant-side technical interpretation (a 'data public defender' or ombudsman with subpoena and re-running capacity), every proposed regime delivers accountability only to plaintiffs wealthy enough to operationalize it.",
      "why_missing": "All three perspectives debate the supply side of proof (who authors the schema, who signs the action, who reads the log) and treat the plaintiff as an abstract rational actor. The Safetyist champions a private right of action but never confronts who funds the plaintiff's expert; the Accelerationist worries about producer-side burden (small labs) but not consumer-side access asymmetry; the Skeptic gestures at 'who can interpret them' but frames it as an institutional design question, not a distributive-access question. None has an incentive to foreground that their preferred mechanism is regressive in access even where it is progressive in form.",
      "gap_type": "blind_spot",
      "relevant_povs": ["safetyist", "skeptic", "accelerationist"],
      "bdi_layer": "desire"
    },
    {
      "argument": "A subject-keyed cryptographic audit trail does not merely fail to explain the model — it actively creates a new surveillance and re-identification infrastructure, since a persistent per-subject key linking every consequential decision made about a person across lenders, courts, and landlords is itself a dossier that can be breached, subpoenaed for unrelated purposes, or repurposed for scoring. The accountability tool and the surveillance harm are the same artifact, and the regime may injure the very subjects it claims to protect.",
      "why_missing": "The Safetyist is committed to the trail as an accountability anchor and frames cryptography as protective, so surfacing it as a surveillance vector cuts against the core proposal. The Skeptic critiques the trail as signal-poor (too little information) but never as dangerously information-rich (a re-identifiable dossier). The Accelerationist favors decentralized verification and would resist centralized keying, but frames the objection around capture/cost, not subject privacy — and none of the three has privacy of the data subject as a primary normative commitment, so the 'subject-keyed' adjective in the resolution goes entirely unexamined as a privacy liability.",
      "gap_type": "unstated_assumption",
      "relevant_povs": ["safetyist", "accelerationist", "skeptic"],
      "bdi_layer": "belief"
    }
  ]
}

