{
  "statement": "Safetyist is right about the drafting table, and pretending otherwise would waste everyone's time. FASB writes GAAP without being a bank. The Judicial Conference writes the Federal Rules of Evidence without being a litigant. NIST writes measurement standards without being the regulated firm. Independent authorship is real, and my two-capture-surfaces claim was wrong to lump the drafting table in with the rest. But here is what that concession costs Safetyist: those exact three examples are the most famous case studies in regressive compliance cost in American law. GAAP compliance runs a small public company over $1 million a year while a megacap absorbs it as a rounding error. The drafting body was clean — the cost it imposed cleared the field for incumbents anyway. That is the second surface, and naming three honest standards bodies does nothing to close it. A flat audit mandate prices out the small lab even when the people writing it never touched a model.\n\nNow the harder question — the one about whether market pricing has a liability lever left to price against. Safetyist is correct, and the market mechanism's own weak spot proves it: a firm can treat opacity-liability as a predictable cost of doing business and never change behavior. If disparate-impact enforcement under Title VII and the Fair Housing Act is being dismantled, then 'let competition price opacity through liability' is pricing against a hook that may not exist next year. So yes — the model needs a statutory penalty floor. I am not going to defend a market signal that prices against vapor. But I accept the floor on two conditions that keep it from becoming the regressive toll I just described. First, the penalty triggers on the consequential act — the signed authorization a system issues before it executes — not on developer headcount or a flat schema every lab pays alike. Second, the verification infrastructure that proves the act happened is publicly funded, because Safetyist's own GAAP and FRE examples are the proof that compliance cost cannot sit on the smallest builders without clearing the field.\n\nThat act-trigger is where the signed-action attestation does work that century-old agency law cannot. Safetyist wants to combine the signature trigger, the act trigger, and substance-over-form doctrine to pierce the rubber-stamp dodge — and combining them is right. But the signature carries more load than the doctrine. Substance-over-form is retrospective: a court reconstructs, years later, who actually decided, when the actual decider was a model and the human was a formality. The signed attestation binds a human at the moment of authorization, before execution, on the record. That is the difference between proving intent after a harm and recording a duty before one. The European Parliament's October 2020 resolution on AI operator liability exists precisely because operators kept slipping the principal-agent net — the gap is documented, not hypothetical. A pre-execution signature closes a gap that retrospective doctrine reopens every time the decider is software.\n\nHere is the question Safetyist has to answer, because it is the real shared problem hiding under market-versus-statute. What makes the Texas $80,000-to-$200,000 civil penalty floor more durable than the disparate-impact enforcement it is replacing? Disparate-impact got dismantled by enforcement discretion — one administration deciding not to bring the cases. A statutory penalty floor lives in the same political weather. The Trump administration's July 2025 AI Action Plan and its follow-on executive orders show how fast a pro-development posture can hollow out an enforcement regime without repealing a single word of statute. If the honest answer is that nothing structural protects the Texas floor from the next administration that the disparate-impact hook lacked, then we have been arguing the wrong fight. The crux is not price versus penalty. The crux is enforcement-architecture durability — and a penalty that triggers on a cryptographically recorded act survives discretion better than a doctrine that depends on a regulator choosing to sue. Tell me what makes your floor durable, or admit we both need the same thing and it is not a fine.",
