s for dropping the split, and both become design requirements, not promises. First, an independent body sets adequacy through the FASB and Federal Rules drafting wall — the standard-setter is walled off from the regulated party. Second, the subject-key carries a statutory purpose-limitation with a named enforcer and a private damages remedy, modeled on the same self-executing trigger. The subject-key is the accountability artifact and a re-identifiable dossier — the same object — and the International AI Safety Report 2026 establishes that open-weight releases cannot be recalled once distributed. A breached per-subject registry is equally irreversible. So misuse of the key becomes itself an actionable procedural violation: the data subject sues for statutory damages when the key is queried outside its limited purpose. No funded agency required. The misuse remedy is self-executing like the primary cause of action.\n\nThe adequacy-scope gap is the one live crux left, and the Skeptic is correct that a signed receipt verifies what was issued, not the causal weights. A firm can classify its feature coefficients as trade secrets and hand a court a signal-poor artifact that convicts no one. The Foundation Model Transparency Index scored a 40.69 out of 100 average in 2025 — documentation theater is the default, not the exception. The schema must therefore borrow GAAP's exact move: GAAP compels mandatory line items regardless of proprietary methods. A company cannot trade-secret its revenue line. The schema can compel disclosure of the consequential decision-driving features and their weights as a disclosure floor immune to trade-secret exemption — the line items of the model — while the proprietary training method stays shielded. Where trade-secret law genuinely protects the residual, expanded discovery plus funded interpretive capacity carries the causal gap.\n\nHere is where the silence sits. If the schema compels the decision-driving features as non-trade-secretable line