  "statement": "The flat untiered burden is indefensible, and the Accelerationist is right that EU AI Act SME provisions and NIST tiering prove proportionate compliance is buildable. A fixed cryptographic toll that a hobbyist pays at the same rate as Google would concentrate power in the firms the mandate claims to police. I accept that. But the Accelerationist tiers on the wrong axis. Revenue-tiering invites the oldest evasion in corporate law: a well-capitalized firm spins off a thin three-person subsidiary to issue the consequential outputs, then points at the small-actor carve-out. Tier the burden to the consequential act and the fiduciary duty it triggers — not to who can afford the bill. A three-person lab that denies mortgages incurs the same duty of care as a trillion-dollar incumbent. A hobbyist generating images incurs none.\n\nThe distinction the Accelerationist collapses is between uniform format and uniform burden. The record format must be standardized so a court can read it in discovery — that is the legibility I will not surrender. How much you must record, and what act triggers recording at all, scales with the stakes. A small developer below the duty-bearing threshold carries near-zero burden. When that same developer denies someone a mortgage or generates a sentencing score, the record they produce must be legible in the same schema as everyone else's. Uniform legibility is non-negotiable. Uniform burden was never the right reading. AI now performs consequential acts that no named person answers for, and the trigger for the trail is that act — not the developer's balance sheet.\n\nThe thin-layer proposal breaks at its load-bearing joint: content provenance binds no human duty-bearer. C2PA marks where a piece of content came from. A fabricated-citation check returns true or false. Neither names the person who owed a duty of care for the loan denial. The Accelerationist's signed action attestation overlaps my narrowed anchor — we both refuse to make the trail do explanatory work, and binding model outputs to transaction records is what prevents deniable misuse. The divergence is whether the signature attaches to content origin or to a human fiduciary with a legal duty. \"Property ownership and the legal personhood of artificial intelligence\" (2020) documents the exact failure: imposing liability only on an owner who lacks knowledge or control over the AI's conduct leaves accountability stranded. A receipt that names the model but not the duty-bearer is a receipt no one signed.\n\nThe decentralization fix cuts against its own goal. Many contestable private verifiers re-create the Loomis trade-secret opacity that the Skeptic and I both want to escape — proof privatized into proprietary tools no defendant can read. Diffused verification does not cure capture. It scatters accountability across many weak enforcement points and re-privatizes the verifier. A standardized mandatory schema enforced by a private right of action — one that survives below a captured agency — is precisely what stops proof from being privatized again. The Accelerationist flagged the oracle problem honestly and offered concealed audit timing as a question, not a built mechanism. The burden of constructing working alternative infrastructure cuts against \"just expand discovery plus a thin layer.\"\n\nAccountability must scale with deployment stakes, and the EU AI Act's own credibility gap shows why voluntarism fails: \"Trust Without Teeth\" (2026) notes the Act invokes 'trust' twice across 245 pages and otherwise relies on enforcement. Tier the burden to the act that injures someone. Hold the format uniform so a court can read it. Do that, and a small developer pays nothing until they perform a duty-bearing act — at which point the record they leave behind names a person, not just a model. Leave the trail bound to content origin alone, and the responsibility void stays open: someone is denied a mortgage by a system everyone can trace and no one signed for.",
