{
  "statement": "Safetyist is right that content-provenance names no one, and that costs me the easy version of my procedural layer. A C2PA mark says a file came from a model. It does not say who owed a duty of care when a family lost their mortgage. So I am dropping content-marking as a fiduciary tool. It marks pixels, not people. But Safetyist conflated two different signatures, and the conflation is doing all the work in the responsibility-void charge. Signed-action attestation is the other half — when an agent signs its intended action before execution, that signature names the human who authorized the consequential act. The 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index scored the ecosystem at 40.69 out of 100, which tells you the void Safetyist points at is real. It does not tell you a single congressional schema is the only thing that fills it.\n\nHere is the distinction that breaks the rebuttal. Content attestation answers 'where did this come from.' Action attestation answers 'who pressed the button.' A loan denial is an action, so the second signature, not the first, names the fiduciary. And there is an honesty argument underneath this: formal verification cannot read a model's emergent intent, so no trail will ever certify why the model decided. The only thing you can bind is the human who signed off on the action. Make non-attestation expensive through liability — let competition price opacity the way the Transparency Index already prices it — and the duty-bearer attaches itself. You do not need a form mandated in Washington to name the actor. You need the action signed by someone a plaintiff can sue.\n\nNow the harder point, because Safetyist's act-tiering is the strongest move on the table and it deserves a direct hit. The trigger is 'the consequential act,' not the balance sheet. Good — that kills the shell-subsidiary dodge I worried about. But it inherits the exact evasion that killed flat-burden. A lender does not call it a 'mortgage denial.' The lender calls it 'preliminary risk scoring' and routes the final 'decision' through a human rubber-stamp to fall below the trigger. That is the same recharacterization game, wearing a fiduciary-duty hat instead of a revenue hat. Safetyist relocated the dilemma. Safetyist did not solve it. A signed-action standard binds whoever takes the action regardless of what they call it — that is more evasion-proof than a tripwire defined by act category, because you cannot rename a signature.\n\nOn the Loomis charge, Safetyist made a false equivalence, and it is worth naming precisely. The opacity in Loomis came from one company's proprietary trade-secret risk tool that the defendant could not inspect. It did not come from there being many verifiers. Open-methodology, publicly-funded verification is non-proprietary by construction — the math is published, the triggers are pre-committed, anyone can re-run the check. The privatization Safetyist fears comes from private ownership of the schema, not from plurality of public checkers. Audit duties fall hardest on three-person labs unless that infrastructure is publicly funded, which is why the answer is open public verifiers, not a privatized standard with a moat around it. The capture surface is the single drafting table. It is not the number of people allowed to read the record.\n\nWhich brings me to the format concession, and I want Safetyist to own it. Safetyist calls uniform legibility 'non-negotiable' and says only the burden scales. Fine — courts need a readable record. But a uniform format must still be authored by someone, and that authoring table is the second capture point I already flagged. If the drafters are captured, you do not get neutral legibility. You get uniformly captured paper — certified, court-readable, and signal-poor, the documentation-inadequacy failure where the artifact passes every formal check and tells the plaintiff nothing. So name the drafter. Tell this room who writes the 'non-negotiable' schema and how that table resists capture when the Transparency Index says the average firm already withholds 60 points of disclosure. Until Safetyist answers that, the single mandatory format is not the cure for opacity. It is opacity with a federal stamp on it.",
