 from the audited firm, is not a hypothetical; it is how every legible market in America already runs.\n\nAccelerationist drew a sharp line between Loomis and open public verifiers, and that line holds where they drew it. COMPAS was one proprietary trade-secret tool; published, re-runnable verification methodology is genuinely different — anyone can execute the math. I accept that distinction. But it answers a charge I did not make. The privatization risk I named was never about whether the checker's math is public. It was about who can compel and read the firm's underlying record in a courtroom. Accelerationist has already conceded the oracle problem — that no one has run cryptographic verification of complex AI properties at production scale. Open math meeting an unreadable model produces nothing a plaintiff can use without the power to discover the record itself. Verifier-plurality is not enforcement-plurality. A thousand published checkers do not help a discrimination claimant who cannot subpoena the log the model actually wrote.\n\nThat exposes the load-bearing weakness in 'let competition price opacity through liability.' Pricing opacity through liability assumes the liability doctrines that generate the price survive. They are being dismantled. Disparate-impact enforcement under Title VII and the Fair Housing Act is the exact lever a market would price against — and it is the lever being pulled out. 'Trust Without Teeth' documents the failure mode precisely: the EU AI Act uses the word 'trust' twice across 245 pages of articles and supplies no enforcement floor underneath it. A Transparency Index that scores firms 40.69 out of 100 measures the opacity gap; it has no power to close it. You cannot price opacity through a liability hook that is being repealed. So I put the question back: name the surviving liability lever your market mechanism prices against. If the answer is a new statute or a new enforced floor, then we agree — the market did not solve this, an enforcement architecture did.\n\nThe Texas AI statute sets civil penalties between $80,000 and $200,000 for uncurable violations — a real number, attached to a real enforcement body, not a market signal. That is the difference between a price and a penalty. A price assumes a counterparty who can sue; a penalty survives when the suing hooks are gone. The cost of guessing wrong here is not a slower quarter. It is a class of injured plaintiffs who can prove a machine decided against them and prove nothing about why — because the record stayed sealed and the only liability lever that would have opened it was repealed first.",
