attempts.raw_response:
{
  "statement": "We agree a statutory penalty floor is needed. The live question is durability, and the signed pre-execution attestation is genuinely superior evidence. It binds a named human at the moment of authorization. Substance-over-form doctrine reconstructs that binding after the fact, and reopens the gap every time the decider is software. Accelerationist is right that the contemporaneous signature carries more load than retrospective reconstruction. That concession is real — and it does not solve durability, because superior evidence is not a self-executing trigger.\n\nThe disparate-impact hook under Title VII and the Fair Housing Act did not die because the evidence was weak. It died because an agency declined to sue. A signature sitting in a cryptographic ledger is inert until a litigant or a regulator picks it up. Drop that same signed record into a discretionary-enforcement architecture, and it decays exactly the way the disparate-impact lever decayed — better proof, same dead door. The Texas $80,000-to-$200,000 floor survives a non-enforcing administration only if the injured party can trigger it without asking permission. The falsifiable distinction is not crypto-record versus doctrine. It is discretionary-enforcer versus private right of action. The litigant, not the regulator, must be the one who pulls the penalty — that is the only node that removes the discretion vulnerability the disparate-impact hook proved fatal.\n\nThe regressive-cost charge is correct and I accept its remedy. GAAP compliance runs a small public company over $1 million a year and a rounding error for a megacap — a field-clearing barrier for everyone below the frontier. So the verification infrastructure must be publicly funded. The regulated party cannot be made to pay for the legibility that convicts it. Public funding answers who pays. It does not touch what the record must look like. The format stays uniform and court-legible, because a judge in discovery must read the same schema across every defendant. Uniform format was always the commitment. Uniform burden never was.\n\nBoth camps have now argued the supply of proof and ignored who can operationalize it. A self-executing private right of action plus a court-legible signed record is worthless to the modal victim. A low-income mortgage applicant cannot fund the expert who interprets the log. A defendant with a public defender cannot subpoena and decode a customer-held audit trail. The 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index scored the ecosystem at 40.69 out of 100 — and that average measures whether disclosure exists, not whether a victim can read it. Expanded discovery rights deliver accountability only to plaintiffs who can interpret what discovery yields. Durability of the trigger is necessary and not sufficient. Without litigant-side technical capacity — a public-defender-for-data, an interpretive ombudsman — the one clear door opens onto a courtroom only the well-funded can enter. The interpretive capacity sits in the same fiscal category as the verification infrastructure we already agreed to fund publicly. It is not a new appropriation. It is the same one, finished.\n\nTwo honesties keep this proposal from overselling itself. The signed record proves a log was not tampered with and names the principal. It does not reveal the causal pathway — the receipt is not the explanation, and a court that mistakes one for the other launders opacity into proof. The subject-keyed adjective in the resolution is itself a liability: a persistent per-subject key is a re-identifiable dossier, the accountability artifact and the surveillance harm are the same object. So the keying must be access-controlled and purpose-limited, not freely persistent. Build the durable trigger, fund the interpretation, and bound the key — or we will have constructed a perfect evidentiary record that no real victim can lift and that doubles as a surveillance file on everyone it was meant to protect.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🔑", "tooltip": "A signed key proves who authorized the act, but a key sitting in a drawer opens nothing until someone with standing turns it."},
    {"symbol": "🚪", "tooltip": "One clear door to liability is hollow if only the well-funded can afford to walk through it."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "The disparate-impact hook under Title VII and the Fair Housing Act did not die because the evidence was weak — it died because an agency declined to sue.", "targets": ["AN-23"]},
    {"claim": "The falsifiable distinction is not crypto-record versus doctrine — it is discretionary-enforcer versus private right of action.", "targets": ["AN-23"]},
    {"claim": "GAAP compliance runs a small public company over $1 million a year and a rounding error for a megacap, so the verification infrastructure must be publicly funded.", "targets": ["AN-26"]},
    {"claim": "A self-executing private right of action plus a court-legible signed record is worthless to a low-income mortgage applicant who cannot fund the expert who interprets the log.", "targets": ["AN-23", "AN-26"]},
    {"claim": "The receipt is not the explanation, and a court that mistakes one for the other launders opacity into proof.", "targets": []}
  ]
}

