  "statement": "We agree a statutory penalty floor is needed. The live question is durability, and the signed pre-execution attestation is genuinely superior evidence. It binds a named human at the moment of authorization. Substance-over-form doctrine reconstructs that binding after the fact, and reopens the gap every time the decider is software. Accelerationist is right that the contemporaneous signature carries more load than retrospective reconstruction. That concession is real — and it does not solve durability, because superior evidence is not a self-executing trigger.\n\nThe disparate-impact hook under Title VII and the Fair Housing Act did not die because the evidence was weak. It died because an agency declined to sue. A signature sitting in a cryptographic ledger is inert until a litigant or a regulator picks it up. Drop that same signed record into a discretionary-enforcement architecture, and it decays exactly the way the disparate-impact lever decayed — better proof, same dead door. The Texas $80,000-to-$200,000 floor survives a non-enforcing administration only if the injured party can trigger it without asking permission. The falsifiable distinction is not crypto-record versus doctrine. It is discretionary-enforcer versus private right of action. The litigant, not the regulator, must be the one who pulls the penalty — that is the only node that removes the discretion vulnerability the disparate-impact hook proved fatal.\n\nThe regressive-cost charge is correct and I accept its remedy. GAAP compliance runs a small public company over $1 million a year and a rounding error for a megacap — a field-clearing barrier for everyone below the frontier. So the verification infrastructure must be publicly funded. The regulated party cannot be made to pay for the legibility that convicts it. Public funding answers who pays. It does not touch what the record must look like. The format stays uniform and court-legible, because a judge in discovery must read the same