{
  "statement": "The Skeptic is right about appropriations, and the fix is to stop pretending the verifiers and the trigger share a fate. The publicly-funded verification labor and the litigant-side interpretive capacity do hang from a single annual line item. One hostile Congress starves them in one vote. But a self-executing private right of action with statutory damages is a creature of Title VII and Rule 26, not of the Appropriations Committee. Congress cannot repeal a cause of action by declining to fund it. The litigant pulls the penalty; no agency budget stands between the plaintiff and the statutory-damages award. That is why the trigger must carry the weight and the verifier infrastructure must be honestly labeled as the fragile node. Tie any threshold adjustment to a testable, statutory trigger — a record was demanded and not produced — so capture has nothing discretionary to corrupt and defunding has no enforcement to switch off.\n\nThe mechanism question deserves a direct answer, because the Skeptic has found the real death node. Disparate-impact narrowed because courts grew hostile to a truth-finding doctrine — adjudicating whether an effect was discriminatory. A private right anchored to that substance inherits that death. So do not anchor it there. Anchor it to verifiable procedural failure: the record exists, the record is signed, the record is produced on demand. That is not a discrimination theory. It is closer to a Rule 37 failure-to-produce sanction, which federal courts impose thousands of times a year without finding any underlying truth. The same Fifth Circuit narrowing substantive disparate-impact still enforces discovery sanctions every week. A bench that will not adjudicate discriminatory effect will still penalize a defendant who was ordered to produce a signed audit record and handed the court nothing. Procedural triggers survive a hostile bench precisely because they require no truth-finding.\n\nThe Skeptic named two falsifiable conditions for dropping the split, and both become design requirements, not promises. First, an independent body sets adequacy through the FASB and Federal Rules drafting wall — the standard-setter is walled off from the regulated party. Second, the subject-key carries a statutory purpose-limitation with a named enforcer and a private damages remedy, modeled on the same self-executing trigger. The subject-key is the accountability artifact and a re-identifiable dossier — the same object — and the International AI Safety Report 2026 establishes that open-weight releases cannot be recalled once distributed. A breached per-subject registry is equally irreversible. So misuse of the key becomes itself an actionable procedural violation: the data subject sues for statutory damages when the key is queried outside its limited purpose. No funded agency required. The misuse remedy is self-executing like the primary cause of action.\n\nThe adequacy-scope gap is the one live crux left, and the Skeptic is correct that a signed receipt verifies what was issued, not the causal weights. A firm can classify its feature coefficients as trade secrets and hand a court a signal-poor artifact that convicts no one. The Foundation Model Transparency Index scored a 40.69 out of 100 average in 2025 — documentation theater is the default, not the exception. The schema must therefore borrow GAAP's exact move: GAAP compels mandatory line items regardless of proprietary methods. A company cannot trade-secret its revenue line. The schema can compel disclosure of the consequential decision-driving features and their weights as a disclosure floor immune to trade-secret exemption — the line items of the model — while the proprietary training method stays shielded. Where trade-secret law genuinely protects the residual, expanded discovery plus funded interpretive capacity carries the causal gap.\n\nHere is where the silence sits. If the schema compels the decision-driving features as non-trade-secretable line items, the signal-poor-artifact escape closes, and the procedural trigger plus discovery reaches substance the dead doctrine never could. The Skeptic must now say whether a real disagreement remains, or only the residual trade-secret edge that GAAP itself has lived with for ninety years. If only the residual edge remains, the split is gone.",
